{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_1",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_1_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "marginalia_0",
      "quote": "Are Machine Learning methods effective in detecting undiagnosed atrial fibrillation in primary care settings using electronic health records? A systematic review",
      "reason": "The report title explicitly includes the phrase “A systematic review,” identifying it as a systematic review."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_1_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "marginalia_0",
      "quote": "Are Machine Learning methods effective in detecting undiagnosed atrial fibrillation in primary care settings using electronic health records?",
      "reason": "The title provides the core review question/objective and specifies the exposure/intervention (machine learning methods), population/setting (primary care), and context/data source (electronic health records) for detecting undiagnosed atrial fibrillation."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_1_e3",
      "status": "MISSING",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "No evidence is provided that the title includes relevant supplementary information such as the analysis method, included study designs, update status, or living-review status."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 72
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_3",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "While evidence on this approach is expanding, it remains unclear how these EHR-based models perform compared to standard care. Many studies lack direct comparisons, and questions remain about the generalizability of ML models in primary care due to variability in validation methods and dataset limitations.",
      "reason": "The current evidence is described as expanding but with explicit uncertainties about performance vs standard care and generalizability/validation limitations."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "Consequently, it is estimated that 20% of AF cases are only diagnosed after a patient experiences their first stroke leading to thousands of hospitalizations per year. Given the high risks associated with untreated AF and the limitations of current screening methods, a more reliable, efficient, and integrated approach is urgently needed in primary care.",
      "reason": "It explains why the review is needed by linking harms of undiagnosed AF and inadequacy of current screening to the need for a better approach."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e3",
      "status": "MISSING",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "The evidence does not explain the need for the current review in the context of similar systematic reviews on EHR-based ML for AF detection, nor does it clearly identify and cite a directly relevant prior systematic review to justify an update/replication."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e4",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_3",
      "quote": "In contrast, our review emphasizes the use of electronic health records, a widely available yet underutilized resource in primary care, for automated risk assessment. In this review, we examined how machine learning models based on electronic health records could improve the detection of undiagnosed atrial fibrillation.",
      "reason": "Although described narratively rather than as an intervention mechanism in trials, it provides the hypothesised mechanism: ML using EHRs to enable automated risk assessment and improve detection."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e5",
      "status": "MISSING",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "There is no logic model presented (or described) showing hypothesised relationships between intervention components and outcomes."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 62
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_4",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e1",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "We conducted a systematic review to evaluate the effectiveness, quality, and applicability of EHR-based ML models for detecting AF in primary care.",
      "reason": "The review objective(s) are explicitly stated, but the evidence does not show that the objectives are framed using a specific relevant question-formulation framework."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "Eligible studies involved adults in primary care where ML models using EHRs were compared to standard care. The primary outcome was the detection of undiagnosed AF;",
      "reason": "For intervention-effect questions, the planned comparison is stated as ML models using EHRs compared to standard care, with the outcome specified as detection of undiagnosed AF."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 78
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_5",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e1",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "The PICOS (population, intervention, comparator, outcome) framework [16] was used to develop the inclusion and exclusion criteria for suitable studies.\n\nWe included peer-reviewed publications in English using the PICOS framework. Population : Studies involving adult populations (aged >18 years) were included. Intervention : Oly studies that developed and/or applied ML methods using data from EHRs or primary care databases to detect new cases of AF within a primary care context were included... Comparison : Standard care practices or validated clinical risk scores for AF. Outcome: The primary outcome of interest was the incidence of new AF diagnoses following the application of ML methods. Secondary outcomes included ... Settings: Eligible studies were conducted within primary care or outpatient settings...",
      "reason": "PICOS components (population, intervention, comparison, outcome, and settings) are specified, but the eligibility description does not explicitly state eligible study designs and does not report any follow-up requirements."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e2",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "We included peer-reviewed publications in English using the PICOS framework.",
      "reason": "Language and publication type/status (peer-reviewed publications) are specified, but dissemination year range and any publication/report status details beyond “peer-reviewed” are not provided in the retrieved evidence."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e3",
      "status": "MISSING",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "The retrieved evidence does not describe a distinction in exclusions between cases where outcomes were not measured versus cases where outcome results were not reported."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e4",
      "status": "MISSING",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "Although the review states it grouped/clusters studies for narrative/descriptive synthesis, the retrieved evidence does not specify the groups used in each synthesis or link them to the comparisons defined in the review objectives."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e5",
      "status": "MISSING",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "The retrieved evidence lists inclusion/exclusion rules (e.g., excluding ML models using ECG/imaging/wearables not integrated into EHR), but it does not provide rationales for notable restrictions on eligibility."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 66
}

